.TH ZXMEDIA 1 "November 2026" "zxkit" "ZxKit"
.SH NAME
zxmedia \- inspect, edit and convert ZX Spectrum tape, disk and Microdrive images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zxmedia
[\fIOPTION\fR...] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENT\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zxmedia
works on image files without starting an emulator.
It lists, extracts, adds, renames, removes and converts the files stored in
tape images (TAP, TZX, PZX, CSW), floppy images (raw IMG, TRD, SCL, MGT, OPD,
D40, D80, CPC DSK and EDSK, TD0, CQM), Microdrive cartridges (MDR), ZX
Spectrum Next files (NEX), FAT12/16/32 SD card images and NextZXOS P3D images.
The floppy filesystems are CP/M, +3DOS, HC BASIC, CIP\-04, TR\-DOS,
MGT/+D/Disciple, Opus Discovery, Didaktik MDOS and CoBra DEVIL.
.PP
Options may be given anywhere on the command line.
Without a command, or with
.BR \-\-help ,
a usage summary is printed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBcreate\fR \fIIMAGE\fR [\fB\-\-boot\-tracks\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Create a blank image.
The format is chosen by the extension of
.IR IMAGE :
.BR .tap ,
.BR .tzx ,
.BR .pzx ,
.BR .csw ,
.BR .trd ,
.BR .scl ,
.BR .mgt ,
.BR .opd ,
.BR .d40 ,
.BR .d80 ,
.BR .dsk ,
.BR .edsk ,
.BR .mdr ,
.B .nex
and
.BR .p3d .
The extensions
.BR .img ,
.B .td0
and
.B .cqm
do not name a filesystem, so they need
.BR \-\-filesystem .
.B .dsk
images are written as Extended DSK.
An existing
.I IMAGE
is an error unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.B \-\-boot\-tracks
installs the system tracks from
.I FILE
in a CP/M style image; they must exactly fill the reserved area (18432 bytes
for CoBra 80K).
A blank formatted image does not contain an operating system.
.TP
\fBlist\fR \fIIMAGE\fR
Print one line per entry.
A directory is shown as
\(lqd 0<TAB>\fINAME\fR\(rq, a file as \(lqf \fISIZE\fR<TAB>\fINAME\fR\(rq
followed by a tab and \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR for each native attribute of the
file.
.TP
\fBget\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Write the contents of
.I ENTRY
to the file
.IR OUTPUT ,
or to standard output when
.I OUTPUT
is
.BR \- .
An existing
.I OUTPUT
file is an error unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
\fBput\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIENTRY\fR [\fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR...]
Store the file
.I INPUT
in
.I IMAGE
as
.IR ENTRY ,
replacing an entry of that name.
The \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR pairs set native attributes (see
.BR ATTRIBUTES ).
.TP
\fBrm\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR
Remove a file.
.TP
\fBrename\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR \fINEWNAME\fR
Rename a file.
An existing
.I NEWNAME
is an error unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
\fBattrs\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR...
Change native attributes of a file.
.TP
\fBmkdir\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR
Create a directory, in the filesystems that have them (FAT).
.TP
\fBboot\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIBOOT_TRACKS\fR
Install the system tracks from the file
.IR BOOT_TRACKS ,
which must exactly fill the reserved tracks of the CP/M style image.
.TP
\fBconvert\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Convert a container or tape to the format given by the extension of
.IR OUTPUT .
Examples are TAP to TZX, PZX or WAV, PZX back to TAP when its standard pilot,
sync and data timings are recognizable, and one floppy container to another.
A TAP becomes an unsigned 8\-bit mono PCM WAV at 44.1 kHz; WAV files cannot be
read.
Arbitrary audio, protected loaders and control flow cannot become ordinary
TAP data, and such conversions fail.
An existing
.I OUTPUT
is an error unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
\fBcopy\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIENTRY\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR \fINEWNAME\fR [\fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR...]
Copy the bytes of
.I ENTRY
from the image
.I INPUT
into the existing image
.I OUTPUT
as
.IR NEWNAME .
Give the attributes the destination filesystem needs explicitly; they are not
translated.
Loaders are not rewritten, so a program does not become compatible with a
different disk interface.
.TP
\fBbasic compile\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Tokenize numbered BASIC text from the file
.I INPUT
into a stored BASIC program
.IR OUTPUT .
.TP
\fBbasic decompile\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Turn a stored BASIC program into an editable text listing.
Token and byte escapes (\fB\e{PRINT}\fR, \fB\exHH\fR) and stored variables
(\fB!raw\fR \fIHEX\fR) are written out, so that compiling the listing again
gives back the original bytes.
.TP
\fBbasic array\-compile\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Convert array text to array data.
The text starts with the comma separated dimensions; numeric values follow as
whitespace separated numbers, and a character array has one quoted
fixed\-width string per row.
.TP
\fBbasic array\-decompile\fR \fIINPUT\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Convert array data to editable array text.
.TP
.B profiles
List the filesystem and geometry profiles accepted by
.BR \-\-filesystem ,
one per line.
.TP
\fBmc\fR \fICOMMAND\fR \fIIMAGE\fR [\fIARGUMENT\fR...]
The extfs protocol of Midnight Commander, used by the zxkit helper of
.BR mc (1).
.I COMMAND
is one of
\fBlist\fR \fIIMAGE\fR,
\fBcopyout\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR \fIFILE\fR,
\fBcopyin\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR \fIFILE\fR,
\fBrm\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR,
\fBrmdir\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR and
\fBmkdir\fR \fIIMAGE\fR \fIENTRY\fR.
.B list
prints
.B ls \-l
style lines.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-filesystem\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
Use the filesystem or geometry profile
.I PROFILE
(see
.BR profiles )
instead of detecting it.
A raw file extension does not uniquely specify a filesystem or geometry.
Existing HC BASIC, CP/M and +3DOS layouts are detected from geometry,
catalogue entries and allocation chains when exactly one profile matches, also
inside DSK, EDSK, TD0 and CQM containers.
Empty raw disks, HC images without a native Spectrum file header and ambiguous
catalogues need this option, and so does creating an
.BR .img ,
.B .td0
or
.B .cqm
image.
An explicit profile always takes precedence; detection never depends on the
directory or name of the image.
The default comes from
.BR ZXMEDIA_FILESYSTEM .
.TP
\fB\-\-dialect\fR \fI48\fR|\fI128\fR|\fInext\fR
The BASIC dialect for
.B basic compile
and
.BR "basic decompile" :
Sinclair 48K (the default), 128K/+3 or NextBASIC.
.TP
\fB\-\-array\-type\fR \fInumeric\fR|\fIstring\fR
Whether
.B basic array\-compile
and
.B basic array\-decompile
work on numeric arrays (the default) or character arrays.
.TP
\fB\-\-label\fR \fITEXT\fR
The volume or cartridge label of a newly created FAT (11 characters), Didaktik
MDOS (10), Opus Discovery (10), TR\-DOS (8) or Microdrive (10) image.
The default is
.BR ZXKIT .
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite existing output files, and an existing destination of
.BR rename .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary.
.SH ENTRY NAMES
Entries are named the way the image stores them, with
.B /
separating directories.
.PP
Tapes show their logical files as
\fBfiles/\fINAME\fR
(duplicate names get numbered aliases) and BASIC programs also as the
writable text view \fBbasic/\fINAME\fB.bas\fR.
\fBblocks/000000.tzx\fR and the following numbers expose the tape blocks one
by one as standalone TZX files.
A block is appended by giving the next sequential number; removing a block
fixes up the jump, call and selection targets of the blocks that remain, and
is refused when a remaining block refers into the removed range.
.PP
A NEX file shows
.BR header.bin ,
\fBbanks/\fINNN\fB.bin\fR for the banks 000 to 111, its loading screens and an
optional
.BR extra.bin .
.PP
CP/M user areas 1 to 15 appear as the directories
.B user01
to
.BR user15 .
Generic CP/M names use upper case, and file lengths are rounded up to 128 byte
records unless a +3DOS or HC BASIC header holds the exact length.
Native names that cannot be POSIX paths are written with percent escapes; a
blank CoBra DEVIL name is
.BR %20 .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
Attributes are given as \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR with a decimal or
\fB0x\fR\-prefixed hexadecimal integer, and are shown by
.BR list .
The names depend on the filesystem.
Tape files and files with a Spectrum header have
.BR type ,
.B param1
and
.BR param2 ;
tape files also have
.BR pause ,
and CP/M files show their user area as
.BR user .
.B readonly
is supported by CP/M, CoBra DEVIL, MGT and FAT,
.B system
and
.B archive
by CP/M and FAT,
.B hidden
by CoBra DEVIL, MGT and FAT.
Didaktik MDOS keeps its native permission bits.
An attribute the filesystem does not have is an error.
The TR\-DOS
.B type
is the ASCII code of its one character extension, for example 67 for
.BR C .
Clear
.B readonly
before modifying or removing a protected file.
.SH SAFETY
Commands that change an image are transactions: the image is read, changed in
memory and written back through a temporary file in the same directory, and a
command that fails leaves the image unchanged.
Writing takes an exclusive advisory lock on the sidecar file
\fIIMAGE\fB.zxmedia.lock\fR
and fails at once with \(lqImage is already being edited\(rq when another
writer holds it.
The image is also checked before it is replaced, and a change made by another
program in the meantime is refused.
The lock file contains no media data and can remain after the process exits.
Do not edit an image in an emulator at the same time.
.PP
The image is kept in memory and cloned for a transaction, so a large SD card
image needs several times its size in free memory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ZXMEDIA_FILESYSTEM
The default for
.BR \-\-filesystem .
Midnight Commander sessions use it to select a profile for ambiguous images.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success.
1 for every error, including usage errors, with a message starting with
\(lqzxmedia:\(rq on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a tape, add a machine code file and read it back:
.PP
.nf
.RS
zxmedia create games.tap
zxmedia put games.tap game.bin files/GAME type=3 param1=32768
zxmedia list games.tap
zxmedia get games.tap files/GAME game.bin
.RE
.fi
.PP
Put a program on a +3 disk, make it read only, and copy a tape file to the
disk:
.PP
.nf
.RS
zxmedia create disk.dsk
zxmedia put disk.dsk program.bin PROGRAM.BIN type=3 param1=32768
zxmedia attrs disk.dsk PROGRAM.BIN readonly=1
zxmedia copy games.tap files/GAME disk.dsk GAME.BIN type=3 param1=32768
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create a FAT SD card image with a directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
zxmedia \-\-filesystem fat \-\-label SPECTRUM create card.img
zxmedia mkdir card.img programs
.RE
.fi
.PP
Convert a tape to audio, and compile BASIC text:
.PP
.nf
.RS
zxmedia convert games.tap games.wav
zxmedia \-\-dialect 128 basic compile source.bas program.bin
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR zxkit\-mc\-setup (1)
